                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "POP: Methodology Experiment One exists as an abundantly expressive creative outlet for an individual who can't find their voice.\n\nThe pulsating lights, mesmerizing soundtrack, and relentlessly bizarre fast cuts of TV clips and weird artwork blend into beautifully incomprehensible chaos as they jump between micro-minigames while seeming to attempt to deliver some sort of message about the cultural insignificance of change. It does all this and yet the result is something remarkably hollow, like it was thrown together just to screw with people believing that it actually has meaning when in reality it's nothing more than a perplexing collection of sights and sounds.\n\nIts handful of insignificant interactive scenes are inexplicably dull for how fast and incomprehensible they are, usually seeming to attempt to again convey a deeper meaning without any context or nuance. And there's something really compelling about that, but at the same time it mostly just had me wondering what I was supposed to take away from something so unfocused trying to deliver a heavy dialogue in a twenty minute compilation of random occurrences and five second slices of gameplay. \n\nMaybe it all just went over my head, or I'm reading too much into something that doesn't exist. I quite frankly haven't the slightest idea.",
